description:
crate-jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:336-348:

  #[non_exhaustive]
  #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
  pub enum MergePatchError {
      /// The patch nests deeper than the crate's `MAX_MERGE_PATCH_DEPTH`
      /// DoS-guard cap.
      DepthExceeded,
  }

MAX_MERGE_PATCH_DEPTH is documented to be 'intentionally not exposed; consumers see the behaviour via the typed error rather than reading the constant' (lines 374-376).

But the variant carries no payload. A consumer that catches DepthExceeded cannot:

1. Log how deep the rejected patch was. (Telemetry / abuse-detection signal: was it 65 or 6500?)
2. Show the user a useful error message ('your patch is too deeply nested — max is N levels'). The constant is gated as private so the consumer cannot even include the cap in the user-facing message.
3. Decide whether to flag the request as malicious (>1000 levels) vs accidentally-deep (66 levels).

A consumer that needs any of these has to either:
- Run their own depth-counting RFC 7396 parser BEFORE calling json_merge_patch (duplicate work).
- Hardcode the same cap value, hoping it matches the crate's internal constant.

The shape that preserves both the 'cap is private' invariant AND the diagnostic information:

  pub enum MergePatchError {
      DepthExceeded {
          /// The depth the patch reached before the cap fired.
          reached: usize,
          /// The cap value enforced. Exposed in the error variant so
          /// callers can include it in user-facing error messages
          /// without depending on a separately-imported pub const.
          max: usize,
      },
  }

Both fields are u-sized, no extra dependency. The cap value being in the variant doesn't break the 'consumers see the behaviour via the typed error' principle — the cap is now ONLY visible through this error, not as a pub const, so the crate can still change MAX_MERGE_PATCH_DEPTH freely between versions and consumers learn the new value via the error.

Or, less invasively, add just `reached: usize` and keep the cap private. Consumers can still log 'depth reached: 67'.

Either fix is non-breaking — adding fields to a struct variant on a #[non_exhaustive] enum is the additive evolution shape per workspace AGENTS.md.

Filed P3 under api-contract,underspecified. The current shape is correct but minimal; the diagnostic information loss is real but not security- or correctness-critical.
